1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,670
Compared to other large mammals on Earth, we humans have relatively little hair.
与地球上的其他大型哺乳动物相比，我们人类的毛发相对较少。

2
00:00:05,670 --> 00:00:14,430
This obvious fact is not so strange until we look at our closest living relatives in the animal kingdom — chimpanzees and other great apes.
这个显而易见的事实并没有什么奇怪的，直到我们看到动物王国中与我们关系最密切的现存近亲——黑猩猩和其他大型猿类。

3
00:00:14,430 --> 00:00:21,380
Why, might we ask, have we evolved to be almost hairless when all other primates are still covered in fur?
我们不禁要问，为什么当所有其他灵长类动物仍然浑身覆盖着毛发时，我们却进化得几乎没有毛发了呢？

4
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:28,840
In fact, bizarre as it may seem, our lack of hair has been the key to our evolutionary success as a species.
事实上，尽管这可能看起来很离奇，但我们毛发的缺失却是我们作为一个物种在进化上取得成功的关键。

5
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:46,860
The question why our earliest ancestors lost their hair when fur would have been beneficial to them — for keeping warm on cold nights, for example — was pondered by Charles Darwin, the famous evolutionist: "No one supposes that the nakedness of the skin is any direct advantage to man."
我们最早的祖先为什么会在毛发对他们有益的时候（比如在寒冷的夜晚保暖）失去毛发，这个问题曾被著名的进化论者查尔斯·达尔文思考过：“没有人认为皮肤裸露对人类有任何直接的好处。”

6
00:00:46,860 --> 00:00:51,220
He concluded that the reason hominins lost their hair was sexual selection.
他得出的结论是，原始人类失去毛发的原因是性选择。

7
00:00:51,220 --> 00:00:54,750
In other words, we prefer our partners to have little hair.
换句话说，我们更倾向于选择毛发较少的伴侣。

8
00:00:54,750 --> 00:00:59,760
But what caused our ancestors to start losing their hair before this preference set in?
但是，是什么导致我们的祖先在这种偏好形成之前就开始失去毛发了呢？

9
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:12,480
The most likely hypothesis to explain the loss of hair is that it became necessary when early hominids moved to a more open savannah habitat around 2 to 3 million years ago, when they started to hunt big game.
解释毛发缺失最有可能的假设是，大约在200万到300万年前，早期原始人类迁移到更开阔的草原栖息地，开始捕猎大型猎物时，毛发的缺失就变得有必要了。

10
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:21,150
Hunting in open savannah meant being exposed to the strong heat of the sun for several hours in the day and being in danger of overheating.
在开阔的草原上狩猎意味着每天要暴露在强烈的阳光下几个小时，并且有过热的危险。

11
00:01:21,150 --> 00:01:25,920
Being covered in hair prevented our ancestors from losing heat fast enough.
浑身覆盖着毛发会阻碍我们的祖先足够快地散热。

12
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:36,200
So, as Peter Wheeler, of Liverpool John Moores University in the UK, explained, losing hair allowed them to develop the ability to sweat and therefore to cool down.
所以，正如英国利物浦约翰摩尔大学的彼得·惠勒所解释的那样，失去毛发使他们能够发展出汗的能力，从而降温。

13
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:40,960
Early humans could, as a consequence, be out at midday hunting and foraging.
因此，早期人类可以在正午时分外出狩猎和觅食。

14
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:51,440
"It would be [an] enormous advantage to be able to spend the entire midday foraging, finding mates or fighting enemies", Tamás Dávid-Barrett of Oxford University says.
牛津大学的塔马斯·大卫－巴雷特说：“能够在整个正午时分觅食、寻找伴侣或与敌人战斗，这将是一个巨大的优势。”

15
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:56,430
"Sweating allows that, and for sweat to be efficient you need to be mostly hairless".
“出汗使这成为可能，而且为了让出汗更有效率，你需要几乎没有毛发。”

16
00:01:56,430 --> 00:02:02,110
The meat from hunting animals gave early humans the energy to fuel their growing brain.
狩猎动物获得的肉为早期人类提供了能量，促进了他们大脑的发育。

17
00:02:02,110 --> 00:02:05,550
Humans are the sweatiest primates on Earth.
人类是地球上出汗最多的灵长类动物。

18
00:02:05,550 --> 00:02:11,140
They have up to 5 million sweat glands, which produce about 12 litres of sweat per day.
人类拥有多达500万个汗腺，每天大约能产生12升汗水。

19
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:16,650
It's this propensity to sweat that accounts for our hairlessness and has allowed us to thrive.
正是这种出汗的倾向解释了我们毛发缺失的原因，并使我们得以繁衍兴旺。

20
00:02:16,650 --> 00:02:21,870
It might help to remember this next time you're surrounded by sweaty hairless torsos in the gym!
下次当你在健身房里被汗流浃背、没有毛发的身躯包围时，记住这一点可能会有所帮助！
